.TH SOMBRA-TRAY 1 "2026-08-09" "sombra" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sombra-tray \- experimentation console for the sombra daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sombra-tray
.RI [ --socket " " path ]
.RI [ --interval-ms " " ms ]
.RI [ --screenshot " " directory ]
.RI [ --quit-after-ms " " ms ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A panel icon and a window with four tabs: the status, the thermal mode, the
backlight, and the settings of the console itself. What it adds over
.BR sombra (1)
is controls that apply the moment they move. Pick a mode and they take its
numbers; move one and it applies at once, and the mode selector empties, because
what is running is then no longer any of the three. One button writes the
controls into a mode you name.
.PP
The window speaks the language of the system, in Russian, English or Spanish, from
the catalogues in
.IR /usr/share/sombra/translations .
Only the window: this program's own
.BR --help ,
the replies of
.BR sombrad (8)
and these manual pages stay in English. An unknown language shows the source
language, which is English.
.PP
Every control explains itself on hover, and one line under the tabs carries both
the daemon's state and its reply to the last command, elided to the room it has
with the whole of it in its tooltip. An accepted reply gives the line back after
five seconds; a refusal stays until the next command.
.PP
Every number has a field beside its slider: a slider cannot be landed on a
particular value, and these values are compared between sessions. The readings
sit on their own tab with three buttons — good, too loud, too slow — which stamp
a judgement made by ear onto the sample it belongs to.
.PP
Nothing here writes the daemon's settings directly. When a live value is worth
keeping, the console asks the daemon to save it, on the same commands
.B sombra config
sends. The one thing the console does write is its own autostart entry.
.PP
The panel icon shows which mode is in force: a chip whose fill rises with the
power envelope, and two colours that are not modes, one for a daemon that is not
answering and one for the emergency guard clamping. It is drawn rather than named
from the icon theme: a StatusNotifierItem host prefers a name over the picture
sent with it and draws a placeholder for one its own theme cannot resolve, and no
icon theme here carries a glyph that means a thermal envelope. The colours are
fixed, because a panel is coloured by the desktop and not by this application's
palette.
.PP
One console per session. Started while another is already running, it asks that
one to open its window and exits: two of them would put two icons in the panel
and two pollers on the daemon, with nothing to say which icon is which. The lock
is a socket in the session's runtime directory, and a leftover from a process
that is gone is taken over rather than waited on.
.PP
Started with a session, it waits up to a minute for a panel to appear before
deciding there is none: the tray host registers over the session bus and is
routinely a second or two behind an autostarted process. Until an icon exists,
closing the console ends the process.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --socket " path"
The daemon's control socket. Default
.IR /run/sombra/control.sock .
Reaching it requires membership of the group
.BR sombra .
.TP
.BI --interval-ms " ms"
How often to ask the daemon for readings. Default 1000.
.TP
.BI --screenshot " directory"
Write the documentation pictures there and exit. Grabs of the console's own
widgets, so no compositor and no screenshot tool is involved. See
.B scripts/screenshots.sh
in the source tree, which is how they are actually taken.
.TP
.BI --quit-after-ms " ms"
Exit after this long. A test hook.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/sombra-tray.sock
The single-instance lock. A second start connects to it, asks the console that
holds it to open its window, and exits.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sombra/console.conf
The console's own settings: at present, whether its window is light, dark or
whatever the desktop says. Nothing about the machine is kept here. Following the
desktop usually means changing nothing at all, because a desktop that manages Qt
applications gives them its own colours already.
.TP
.I /usr/share/sombra/help/sombra-help.\fIlanguage\fP.html
The reference behind the
.B ?
at the end of the status line: every control of every tab with its units, its
range and what it costs, opened at the section of the tab the button was pressed
on. One file per language the console speaks, chosen the way a translation is —
the system's language, then English.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/autostart/sombra-tray.desktop
Whether the console starts with the session. The entry is the whole state,
which is what makes "switched off and it stayed off" a property of the
arrangement rather than of a mechanism. The package writes nothing here.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sombra (1),
.BR sombrad (8)
